  "analysis": "Applied Digital's financial health is critically impaired on fundamental metrics despite a surface-level improvement in liquidity. The company has not generated positive free cash flow in any of the eight quarters surveyed, and the FCF burn rate has accelerated — from -$190M in Q4 FY25 to -$720M in Q3 FY26 — as the company executes an aggressive CapEx program averaging roughly $450M per quarter [Source: FMP /cash-flow-statement]. The $1.73B cash balance as of Q3 FY26 represents a significant jump from $70M just two quarters prior, which almost certainly reflects debt issuance or equity capital raises rather than operational generation [Source: FMP /balance-sheet Q3 vs Q1]. At the Q3 CapEx run rate of $780M/quarter, even this cash cushion provides only approximately 2 quarters of runway before external financing becomes mandatory.\n\nThe debt structure is deeply opaque. Long-term debt has grown from $310M in Q1 FY26 to $2.66B in Q3 FY26 — an increase of $2.35B in just two quarters [Source: FMP /balance-sheet]. The XBRL filings disclose no maturity schedule, no weighted average interest rate, and no covenant terms [Source: SEC EDGAR XBRL — not disclosed]. The reported 'latest interest expense: $0.00B' is arithmetically implausible on $2.66B of debt and suggests either capitalization of interest, a grace period structure, or a data reporting lag — each carrying different risk implications. At a conservative 6% blended rate, annualized interest would run ~$160M against a revenue base of approximately $520M annualized, consuming roughly 30% of top-line. This ratio would be serviceable only if EBITDA margins improve substantially from current levels.\n\nGross margin progression is the one genuinely positive data point: the trajectory from negative territory 18 months ago to 42.5% in Q3 FY26 confirms real pricing power and scale economics in the HPC/cloud segment [Source: FMP /income-statement]. However, operating margin remains -15% and net margin -78.4% in